Z||000003||kslish||02-16-2001||08:08 AM||||I am a part-time demonstration rep for Apple, so anything I say isn't endorsed by the company, yada-yada-yada.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>Anyways, here is the easy (short) answer.  Buy a Mac.  There is no emulator that I know of for the PC that will boot MacOS 8.6.  All the latest PC emulators in existance now emulate the 68040 Motorola Processor which can only run up to MacOS 8.1.  MacOS'es after 8.1 require a Mac with a PowerPC processor.  You can probably pick up a first generation PowerPC Mac (6100 or 7100 series) that will run OS 8.6 for $200 or probably much, much less (it's been a while since I priced them used).  They'll plug right into your PC monitor with a cheap adaptor.<P>Okay, the long answer.  Here are the available Mac emulators for the PC:<P>That Mac Plus emulator, but that is ancient technology....will that really help you?  <P>-----------<BR>Next up, Executor:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.ardi.com/executor/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ardi.com/executor/index.html</A> <P>Pros: A free 30 day demo is available.  No ROM dump needed.  Includes the operating system.  <BR>Cons: Expensive at $150 (better to get a real Mac for this price) and emulates a 68040 Mac which will only run up to OS 8.1.<P>------------<BR>Basilisk II<BR> <A HREF="http://www.uni-mainz.de/~bauec002/B2Main.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.uni-mainz.de/~bauec002/B2Main.html</A> <P>Pros: Free and its open source software.  <BR>Cons: Not full featured since it is only version 0.81 (pre-release software that may never be fully completed).  You need a ROM dump and a copy of the MacOS.  Though it may be a lot easier to find a ROM dump of a 68040 (Quadra) machine than a Plus since they are probably still around in a lot of places.  And yet again, can only run up to MacOS 8.1.<P>-------------<BR>SoftMac 2000<BR> <A HREF="http://www.emulators.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.emulators.com/</A> <P>From what I have heard, it's the best that is out there for now.  <P>Pros: Fast, 30-day free demo version available.  Looks like it's pretty full featured.<BR>Cons: Costs $69.95, requires a ROM dump from a Mac.  Will only run up to OS 8.1 since it's yet another 68040 emulator.<P>They also have made their previous Mac emulator, Fusion 3.0 downloable for free.  It runs in DOS and I'm not sure what MacOS it tops out at.<P>So there you have it.  Still determined to run the MacOS on your PC?  <P>By the way, the BSD Unix-derived MacOS X comes out March 24th, hope they are training you for the future, being knowledgable about Unix or Linux will probably help you on that one.<P>Ken S.||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroSTETZ||02-16-2001||11:05 AM||||well, my main point for having mac os on the machine is to get service source to run on my laptop- already bugged our local apple rep and now i have a copy of OS X- but nothing to run it on. my nec laptop currently boots into os 7.6 or 7.8 (not sure) but was hoping for a way to get a later os so SS would run... gonna try to find a power book... thanks for responses!||||reg||1
Z||000005||MontanaFiero||02-17-2001||04:56 AM||||KSLISH-- My 7100 won't run 8.5 It says I need a G3 or better to run or install it. The most I have been able to go is 8.1<P>NOW you said it would run 8.6-- is this true? I noticed 9.1 is available to install on those machines if you use the cd (according to the installation notes).||||reg||1
Z||000006||kslish||02-17-2001||11:47 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MontanaFiero:<BR><B>KSLISH-- My 7100 won't run 8.5 It says I need a G3 or better to run or install it. The most I have been able to go is 8.1<P>NOW you said it would run 8.6-- is this true? I noticed 9.1 is available to install on those machines if you use the cd (according to the installation notes).</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I used to have a 7100 that ran 8.5-8.6 just fine before I sold it and got a G3.<P>Question #1: Do you have an copy of 8.5 or 8.6 that is for installation on all Macs?  Not a copy that was say, bundled with another computer but the full retail version?  Apple sometimes "handicaps" the system disks so they will only install on the type of computer they were bundled with.<P>Question #2: Is your computer an actual PowerMac 7100 or a Power Computing clone?  (I noticed you said you were maybe looking to sell one above.)<P>The reason I am asking is the retail version of OS 8.5-8.6 will run on a clone, but Apple didn't feel like supporting the non-Apple ROM disk drives and CD drives built into clones so the installer balks when it sees non-Apple hardware.  They are ways around it, nothing to terrible just annoying.<P>However, I would recommend staying with OS 8.1 on a 7100.  It uses less memory than OS 8.5 and above and also runs faster on slower processors.<P>Ken S.<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by kslish (edited 02-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Songman||02-17-2001||12:24 PM||||There are actually quite a few Mac emulators available. Since I came from the Mac world to PC I looked for them too...<P>Probably the best one that stays up to date is called Fusion. Not sure how forward it goes on OSs though...<P>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000008||MontanaFiero||02-17-2001||03:46 PM||||kslish--<BR>I guess I was using the download (installing from a zip disk), what I really needed was the CD, right?<P>I have the Power Computing clone, and a 7100, as well as my iMac. But you say the performance of the more memory intensive OS's sucks on these machines?||||reg||1
Z||000009||beer_stud_21||02-17-2001||10:42 PM||||MontanaFiero,<P>The older macs can work great with enough memory installed.  try getting the installed ram up to AT LEAST 32mb, anything less than that will make the computer a *****  to use.  My 8500 has 176mb and that is plenty for what I do even  using OS 9 w/VM off.  I do web design/graphics stuff mostly.   As far as the mac emulator goes....  My vote goes for picking up a cheap pci powermac, sticking some ram in it (the ram is pretty expensive, but you should be able to get 64mb (one chip) for under or around $100.  Try this:<P>PM 7500/7600/8500:  Price $120-250<BR>-price depends on if its stripped or not.<P>if you get one of these computers with 120-150mhz go find a cheap G3 card or for less than $30 you should be able to find a 233mhz 604e card, and if your lucky a 1mb cache card. -my vote would be for the g3, way faster and just better all around.<P>Hook up your pc monitor speakers etc.<P>I always like talking to mac folk.  I was a tech support rep for Apple for about 9-10 months a little less than a year ago.  Apple had the tech support sub contracted back then and I left when they pulled it all back in house.  Maybe some of you talked to me if you ever called tech support.. hehe  Anyways, if you have any questions or want to talk my icq number is 8273989 and e-mail is marlinschaefer@mac.com   I also just bought my first fiero. A non running 85 GT auto.  <P>-peace out all...<BR>Marlin||||reg||1
Z||000010||MontanaFiero||02-18-2001||04:22 AM||||kslish--<BR>I guess I was using the download (installing from a zip disk), what I really needed was the CD, right?<P>I have the Power Computing clone, and a 7100, as well as my iMac. But you say the performance of the more memory intensive OS's sucks on these machines?||||reg||1
Z||000011||kslish||02-19-2001||08:06 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MontanaFiero:<BR><B>kslish--<BR>I guess I was using the download (installing from a zip disk), what I really needed was the CD, right?<P>I have the Power Computing clone, and a 7100, as well as my iMac. But you say the performance of the more memory intensive OS's sucks on these machines?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Download?  If you are referring to the downloadable OS 8.6 update installer your problem may be right there.  The upgrader will only upgrade a machine with OS 8.5 installed (so you can't go directly from OS 8.1 to 8.6).<P>If you want to upgrade to OS 8.6, find a copy of OS 8.5 on CD (that installer is too big to fit on a Zip as far as I know), upgrade to that and then run the OS 8.6 updater from a Zip or whatever.<P>MacOS 8.5 CD's can usually be found used for around $20 or so.  <P>Oh, and the performance of OS 8.5 and up on 7100 series Macs doesn't so much "suck" if you have sufficient memory installed like previously mentioned by another poster, but I've noticed that normal everyday Finder operations feel a little less "snappy" in the newer OSes which I find annoying.  However, pop in a G3 processor upgrade and all is well again.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Ken S.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||FieroSTETZ||02-21-2001||02:24 AM||||can anyone hook me up with a rom dump from a mac plus or a 608040 based computer? i need to be able to run os 8.1 so i can run service source- i dont have the engine to rebuild my engine with rod knock right now, much less buy another computer. thanks for any response!||||reg||1
